      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The skeletal system is the internal framework of the human body. It provides
    shape, support, and stability and helps protect important organs. An adult human
    skeleton normally contains 206 bones, which vary in size and shape depending
    on their location and function.

    The skull forms the framework of the head
    and protects the brain. The vertebral column, or spine, supports the upper body
    and protects the spinal cord. The rib cage surrounds important organs in the
    chest, including the heart and lungs. The pelvis supports the upper body and
    connects the spine with the lower limbs.

    The upper limbs contain several
    important bones. The humerus is the main bone of the upper arm, while the radius
    and ulna form the forearm. The hands contain many smaller bones that allow a
    wide range of movements. In the lower limbs, the femur is the large bone of the
    thigh and is the longest and strongest bone in the human body. The patella, or
    kneecap, is located at the knee. Below the knee are the tibia and fibula, which
    form the lower leg. The feet also contain many bones that help support the body.

    Bones
    meet at structures called joints. Many joints allow different bones to move in
    relation to one another. Working together with muscles, the skeleton enables
    activities such as standing, walking, bending, and lifting.

    Together, the
    bones and joints of the skeletal system form a strong framework that supports
    the body, protects vital organs, and helps produce movement.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 6468205213134445944, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
